There is something just about normal human psychology that makes misconceptions so sticky, that the way our memory works, for example, when we hear things over and over and over again, even when it's not true, even if we're told this is not true, repeated exposure increases our belief that that thing is true. And this matters when we kind of go down the line and want to reason of it. This is also a little bit of foreshadowing. What makes debunking so difficult is that sometimes when you are attempting to debunk something by stating very clearly this misconception that you're trying to debunk, you actually might be reinforcing that misconception because now you're increasing exposure to it. That's normal. If we think about when we raise children and we tell them

